# MinUI Project Context

## Project Overview
MinUI is a focused, custom launcher and libretro frontend for retro gaming handhelds. It provides a simple, no-frills interface for playing retro games across multiple devices from different manufacturers.

## Key Features
- Simple launcher with minimal configuration
- Libretro frontend (minarch) with custom cores
- Cross-device compatibility (single SD card works on multiple devices)
- Auto-sleep, auto-resume, and power management
- Save state support with quick resume
- Consistent in-emulator menu system

## Project Structure

### Build System
- `makefile` - Main build orchestration (runs on host system, not in Docker)
- `makefile.toolchain` - Toolchain and Docker build configuration
- `commits.sh` - Generates commit history for releases
- `workspace/makefile` - Platform-specific builds

### Platform Support
The project supports multiple retro handheld devices via platform-specific folders in `workspace/`:
- `miyoomini/` - Miyoo Mini family (Mini, Mini Plus, Mini Flip)
- `trimuismart/` - Trimui Smart devices
- `rg35xx/`, `rg35xxplus/` - Anbernic RG35xx series
- `my355/`, `my282/` - Miyoo A30, Flip devices
- `tg5040/` - Trimui Smart Pro
- `rgb30/` - Powkiddy RGB30
- `m17/` - M17 device
- `gkdpixel/` - GKD Pixel
- `zero28/` - MagicX Mini Zero 28
- `magicmini/` - MagicX XU Mini M
- `macos/` - Development/testing on macOS

### Core Components (workspace/all/)
- `minui/` - Main launcher UI
- `minarch/` - Libretro frontend (custom RetroArch-like implementation)
- `syncsettings/` - Sync brightness/volume settings
- `clock/` - Clock tool
- `minput/` - Input configuration tool
- `say/` - Text-to-speech utility
- `common/` - Shared code (api.c, scaler.c, utils.c)

### Platform-Specific Components
Each platform folder typically contains:
- `platform/` - Platform-specific code (display, input, power management)
- `libmsettings/` - Settings management library
- `keymon/` - Key monitoring daemon
- `init/` - Initialization code
- `show/` - Show/display utilities
- `cores/` - Platform-specific libretro cores

### Distribution Structure (skeleton/)
- `BASE/` - Base installation files (README, boot scripts, MinUI.zip payload)
- `BOOT/` - Boot-time files organized by device family (miyoo, trimui, magicx)
- `EXTRAS/` - Optional emulators and tools
  - `Emus/[platform]/[TAG].pak/` - Emulator paks (e.g., MGBA.pak, P8.pak)
  - `Tools/[platform]/` - Tool paks (Clock, Input)
- `SYSTEM/[platform]/` - Platform-specific system files (hidden on device)

## Supported Consoles

### Base Support
- Game Boy, Game Boy Color, Game Boy Advance
- NES, SNES, Sega Genesis, PlayStation

### Extras
- Neo Geo Pocket (Color), Sega Game Gear, Sega Master System
- Pico-8, Pokémon mini, Super Game Boy
- TurboGrafx-16 (CD), Virtual Boy

## Key Technologies
- C programming language
- Libretro API for emulation cores
- SDL (Simple DirectMedia Layer) for some platforms
- Platform-specific framebuffer and input APIs
- Docker for cross-compilation toolchains

## PAK System
MinUI uses a "pak" system for emulators and tools:
- A pak is a folder with `.pak` extension containing a `launch.sh` script
- Emulator paks live in `Emus/[platform]/[TAG].pak/`
- Tool paks live in `Tools/[platform]/`
- Paks are platform-specific
- See PAKS.md for detailed documentation on creating paks

## Build Process
1. `make setup` - Prepares build directory from skeleton
2. `make [platform]` - Builds platform-specific binaries
3. `make system` - Copies system files to build directory
4. `make cores` - Copies libretro cores
5. `make special` - Sets up device-family-specific boot structures
6. `make package` - Creates release ZIP files

## Important Files
- `README.md` - User-facing documentation
- `PAKS.md` - Developer guide for creating emulator/tool paks
- `skeleton/BASE/README.txt` - Installation instructions
- `skeleton/EXTRAS/README.txt` - Extras installation guide
- `workspace/hash.txt` - Git commit hash for current build

## Development Notes
- All platforms deprecated as of 2025 (see README device table)
- Build system runs on host, not in Docker (prevents docker-in-docker)
- Uses device-specific toolchains via makefile.toolchain
- Supports HDMI output on some devices via `DEVICE` environment variable
- Platform identification via internal names (e.g., tg5040 for Trimui Smart Pro)

## Common Patterns
- Platform code uses framebuffer manipulation for display
- Input handled via device-specific event systems
- Power management includes auto-sleep (30s) and auto-poweroff (2min)
- Save states managed automatically with resume support
- Global brightness/volume synced via syncsettings.elf

## Testing
- macOS workspace available for development testing
- Each platform requires real hardware for final testing
- SD card compatibility testing across multiple devices
